For every fixed $L_0\ge1$ and every sufficiently small fixed $e_0>0$, a matrix $B$ in the bit model of size $m=N-1$ satisfies, for all sufficiently large $N$, $$\mathbb P\left(
 B\text{ is nonsingular and }
 |\mathcal S_{L_0}(B)|>2^{e_0N}
 \right)
 \le 2^{-N(2a-10e_0)}.$$

For every lattice $L$ and $T>0$, the quantity $$\frac{\det M}{T^{\mathop{\mathrm{rank}}M}},\qquad M\subseteq L\text{ a sublattice},$$ attains its minimum at a saturated sublattice $L_{\min}$. It satisfies $$\det L_{\min}\le T^{\mathop{\mathrm{rank}}L_{\min}},\qquad
 \det M\ge T^{\mathop{\mathrm{rank}}M}
 \quad\text{for every sublattice }M\subseteq L/L_{\min}.$$

There is a fixed $c_3>0$ such that, for every integer $1\le|u|\le N^C$ and every collection of real numbers $(s_i)_{i\in J}$, $$\label{eq:lattice-fourier-average}
 \mathbb E_y\prod_{i\in J}|\varphi(uy_i+s_i)|^2
       \le e^{-c_3\tau N}.$$

Let $B$ be a nonsingular symmetric integer matrix. For each prime $l$, put $f_l=b_{l,3}$. The perfect pairing on $G_{B,l}$ admits an orthogonal decomposition $$G_{B,l}=U_l\oplus V_l,$$ where $U_l$ consists of the blocks with cyclic exponents strictly greater than $f_l$, and the exponent of $V_l$ divides $l^{f_l}$. There are at most two cyclic factors in $U_l$. The isotropic elements of $G_{B,l}$ can be colored with $C_l$ colors satisfying $$\label{eq:primary-color-count}
 C_l\le1+8b_{l,1},
 \qquad C_l\le2^{b_{l,2}-b_{l,3}}\quad\text{if \(l\) is odd},$$ such that two elements of one color satisfy $$\label{eq:primary-color-pairing}
 l^{f_l}\lambda_B(x_{U_l},y_{U_l})=0
 \quad\text{in }\mathbb Q/\mathbb Z.$$ If $X\subset G_B$ consists of isotropic elements with one fixed color at every prime, put $H=\langle x-y:x,y\in X\rangle$. Then the image of the *restricted* pairing map $$\label{eq:restricted-pairing-map}
 H\longrightarrow\operatorname{Hom}(H,\mathbb Q/\mathbb Z),
 \qquad u\longmapsto\lambda_B(u,\cdot)|_H,$$ has order at most $N^{NJ_B}$.

Fix $0<\eta<1$, and put $q_{\min}=\eta/(100\log N)$. There is a constant $c_p>0$ such that, uniformly for $q_{\min}\le q\le1/2$, $$\label{eq:path-heavy-cost}
 \mathbb P\bigl(Q_{\rm hi}\in[q,2q)\mid\text{previous exposure}\bigr)
       \le2^{-c_pqN}.$$ This holds whenever the previous exposure fixes a nonsingular start $C$ of size in $\mathcal I_N$, and a quotient map to $G$ whose kernel contains $C\mathbb Z^m$, while leaving the tested bits independent with success probabilities $p$ or $b$.
